  "live_records": {
    "q-138535": {
      "dom_id": "step-q138535",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & 0 & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$",
      "worked_solution": "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of $Σ.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}^{+}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{10} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & =V\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{10} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{10} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{10} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
