    "q-138563": {
      "dom_id": "step-q138563",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$",
      "worked_solution": "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of $Σ.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n{Σ}^{+}=\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}^{T}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & =V\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-138563-a",
